purpose: CSS tokenizing, parsing, selectors. Spec-literal, because CSS error recovery is normative.

# `text` is here for one question: `dir="auto"` is UAX #9's rule P2 applied to an element's own
# text, and "which direction is this text?" is a character-property question -- which is exactly what
# `src/text` exists to answer and the only kind of question it can answer. It cannot see a box, a
# font or a document, so nothing about layout can reach the cascade through it.
allow: util dom gfx text

public: Token.h Tokenizer.h StyleSheet.h Cssom.h ComputedStyle.h SvgStyle.h Length.h Transform.h Timing.h Animation.h StyleResolver.h MediaQuery.h DeclarationText.h

extern:

max_tu_lines: 900

# The scanner counts the nested `enum class Kind` as a class of its own. Useful
# rather than a false positive: it is the spec's token list, and a token kind
# added without a parser branch for it is a silent gap.
# 35 -> 55 for the four kinds D5 added -- `Has`, `Scope`, `Lang` and `Dir` --
# and their reasoning. Each is a *different shape* of selector rather than one
# more name: `:has()` is the only one that searches a subtree, `:scope` the only
# one whose answer depends on what the match was asked about, and `:lang()`/
# `:dir()` the only two that ask about content rather than tree shape. A kind
# per shape is what keeps the matcher a switch the compiler checks.
budget: Kind             header_lines=55 public_methods=0  members=0
# One member for `has_sign`: whether a numeric token was written with an
# explicit sign. The An+B microsyntax of `:nth-child()` is the one CSS grammar
# where `+3` and `3` are not the same token, and the difference cannot be
# recovered from the value once the tokenizer has dropped it.
# One more for `quote`: a string token's opening quote. Custom-property
# computed values must round-trip `'a'` vs `"a"`, and the quote is gone from
# `value` once the tokenizer has taken it off.
budget: Token            header_lines=82 public_methods=2  members=9
# Two members for the functional pseudo-classes of ADR 0016 §1: a nested
# selector list for `:is()`/`:where()`/`:not()`, and the An+B of `:nth-child()`.
# The ADR predicted this budget would fire and said why it should: a selector
# list inside a selector list is a new kind of thing to store, and it is what
# turns matching from a loop into a recursion.
# Two more kinds for ADR 0019: `:host`/`:host(sel)` and `::slotted(sel)`, which
# are the only two selectors that reach out of the tree they were written in.
# +1 kind for `::before`/`::after` generated content (youtube thumbnail aspect hack).
# 70 -> 105 and 8 members for D5. The two new members are the `i`/`s` flag of
# `[att=val i]` and the namespace constraint of `*|att`, and both are *three*
# valued rather than boolean, which is the point: "the author wrote `s`" and
# "the author wrote nothing" are different, because HTML matches a list of
# attribute values case-insensitively by default and only `s` turns that off.
# Folding them into one packed byte would have kept the count and lost the
# names, which is the wrong trade in the struct the whole cascade reads.
# `:has()` and `:lang()` added no member: the first reuses `arguments`, the
# second packs its ranges into `value`.
# +1 member for the prefix of `ns|e` / `[ns|attr]`. CSSOM serializes the prefix
# the author wrote; matching it without a resolved URI would style the wrong
# elements, so Named matches nothing until the cascade grows `@namespace`.
budget: SelectorPart header_lines=115 public_methods=2 members=9
budget: NthPattern       header_lines=20 public_methods=2  members=4
budget: CompoundSelector header_lines=25 public_methods=2  members=2
budget: Specificity      header_lines=30 public_methods=4  members=3
# Raised for DynamicStates, which is the key an invalidation index files a rule
# under. A method on the selector rather than a walk inside the index, so that
# the two cannot disagree about which pseudo-class name means which state --
# a rule filed under a name nothing sets is a rule that never re-applies.
# +SubjectPseudoElement for ::before/::after.
budget: Selector         header_lines=50 public_methods=7  members=1
# Three members for `calc()`: a coefficient, the unit it is a coefficient of,
# and the absolute pixels added to it. `calc(100% - 20px)` is the form the
# feature is overwhelmingly used in and it is two terms, so a one-term Length
# could only have stored it by rounding one of them away. The alternative --
# a full expression tree per length -- would have put an allocation on the type
# four of which sit in every Edges, for a grammar whose measured depth is one.
budget: Length           header_lines=45 public_methods=10 members=3
# One member per property, plus one grouped member for the twelve flex
# properties. Twelve loose fields would have been more than a third of this
# struct and would have said nothing about belonging together; grouped, the
# member count went up by one and the header by the description of what the
# container reads and what the item reads.
# A property bag, and it grows one member per CSS property this engine
# supports -- which is the point of it. The member count is therefore a measure
# of coverage rather than of design, and the numbers that say whether this is
# becoming something other than a property bag are the method count and whether
# any group of fields is large enough to want a struct of its own. Two already
# do: `background` and `flex`.
# Grew by one member and two methods for custom properties, which are a new
# *kind* of stored value rather than one more property: unparsed text, kept as
# text because what `--x: 20px` means depends on where it is substituted. ADR
# 0014 has the measurement that made them the first CSS work to do.
# One method and its reasoning for `display: inline-block`: IsAtomicInline,
# which carries the blockification rule -- a float or an absolutely positioned
# box is not on a line at all, so it is not an atomic inline whatever it was
# declared as. It sits beside IsInlineLevel, which states the same rule for the
# same reason: applied at each call site, one of them eventually forgets.
# +13 lines and +3 members for ADR 0014 §4's `transform`: the operation list plus the
# two halves of `transform-origin`. The list itself is `css::TransformList` in its own
# header rather than fields here -- see Transform.h for why it stays a list of
# operations instead of a matrix.
# The custom properties in scope on one element, copy-on-write. One member and
# it is a `shared_ptr`, which is the whole design: inheriting is the operation
# that happens most -- every element takes its parent's whole set -- and a page
# that declares its palette on `:root` was copying fifty string pairs into each
# of 19,000 elements per cascade pass. That was 24 of the 30 seconds
# en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CSS took after the font fix. The lines are the sharing
# rule and the content-comparing `operator==` that keeps ComputedStyle's
# defaulted one from silently becoming a pointer comparison.
budget: CustomProperties header_lines=70 public_methods=8 members=1
# +2 members for `visibility` and `pointer-events` (youtube closed drawer +
# yt-interaction ink layers that must not steal clicks from the thumbnail link).
# +1 member for `box-sizing` (iron-fit's border-box max-height on youtube consent).
# +1 member for `opacity` (paint-only; youtube's yt-interaction .fill and
# iron-overlay-backdrop are `background:black; opacity:0` / translucent — without
# the property those layers paint solid black over the page).
# Raised from 340 when the border became four sides rather than one: `BorderSides`,
# `BorderColors`, and the two accessors that answer "does this side draw?" and "what colour is a
# side that never said?" -- both of which exist so that the eleven call sites that used to read
# `has_border ? border_width : Edges{}` cannot each get the four-way version wrong.
# Two more members when `white-space` became two properties (`white-space-collapse` and
# `text-wrap-mode`), which is what CSS Text 4 made it and what the suite tests: `pre-line` and
# `break-spaces` are combinations of the two halves rather than values of one enum, so a single
# field could not spell them.
# 42 -> 44 for `letter-spacing` and `word-spacing`. Two lengths rather than one pair on a
# sub-struct: they inherit separately, they are set by separate declarations, and the one place
# both are read (`FontRequestFor`) reads them as two numbers. A struct holding them would be a
# name for nothing.
# 44 -> 45 for the text of a string `content`. A member rather than a second enum value, because
# `content: "x"` has to carry the x somewhere and every other place to put it -- a side table keyed
# by element, a re-parse at box-tree time -- would be a second copy of a value the cascade already
# resolved, with a second chance to disagree with it. The header lines are the paragraph saying why
# `Empty` stays a value of its own rather than becoming a String holding "".
# 45 -> 46 members for ADR 0043 §2, the SVG presentation properties. One member,
# not twenty-two: `SvgStyle` is grouped for the reason `FlexStyle` and
# `GridStyle` are, and it lives in `SvgStyle.h` because this header is at the
# module's 900-line cap -- a file over its cap means a missing header, not a
# bigger one. Nine enums rather than one shared "SVG keyword" enum, because a
# value that belongs to one property must be refused on the others, and a shared
# enum makes that a check somebody has to remember instead of a type the
# compiler enforces.
# +2 members for CSS Transforms 2's individual transform properties and
# `transform-box`. The three properties are one grouped member in Transform.h,
# beside the operation type they are made of; `transform-box` is one enum.
budget: BackgroundLayer  header_lines=30  public_methods=2  members=7
budget: ComputedStyle    header_lines=382 public_methods=27 members=48
# Every field inherits, so the struct is copied whole by `InheritInto` -- which
# is why a field added here is inherited by default and why that default is
# correct for this group and for no other.
# The third "method" is `kPaintOrderNormal`, the packing `normal` and
# `fill stroke markers` share. A constant rather than a zero of its own, because
# the two spell the same order and nothing downstream may tell them apart.
budget: SvgStyle         header_lines=75  public_methods=3  members=22
budget: Paint            header_lines=20  public_methods=2  members=3
budget: TransformOperation header_lines=50 public_methods=2 members=9
budget: TransformList    header_lines=60 public_methods=4 members=1
budget: IndividualTransforms header_lines=45 public_methods=3 members=3
budget: Length           header_lines=40 public_methods=8 members=3
budget: FlexStyle        header_lines=50  public_methods=2  members=12
# The third member is the invalidation index of ADR 0016 §3: which dynamic
# states any rule in this cascade depends on, and which of those can move a box.
# It is on the resolver because it is a property of *the cascade* rather than of
# any one sheet -- the question a change asks is "could anything have changed",
# and one index per sheet would mean asking it once per sheet.
# 95 lines, 18 methods and three members for ADR 0019's scoped cascade. The two
# new methods are static and that is the claim: `ScopeOf` and `ScopeAdmits` are
# questions about a pair, kept *out* of `Selector::Matches` because that is a pure
# function of (element, selector) -- ADR 0016's rule -- and a scope is neither an
# element nor a selector. An Entry carries the shadow root it came from; null is
# the document, so the common comparison costs a pointer compare.
# 95 -> 130 and a fourth member for the rule index. The cascade matched every
# element against every rule, which is quadratic in the page and was 99% of the
# time to load youtube.com -- 29.1 of 29.2 seconds, with the box placement it
# was hiding behind measuring 22ms. The index is a member rather than a free
# function because it has to be built as sheets are added and consulted per
# element, and those are the resolver's two jobs; the header lines are the
# explanation of what may be filed where, which is the part that has to be
# right, since a rule filed under a key its element never asks for does not
# render *sometimes* -- it stops applying.
# +1 member for the viewport lengths in declarations resolve against. Folded at
# cascade time rather than stored on Length, because a length that survives a
# resize would be wrong — the same reason @media re-parses on SetViewport.
# +3 members for the TD-0021 computed-style cache (map, next id, generation stamp).
# header_lines cover StyleFor's provenance parameters and the cache comment.
# +1 method and +1 member for `::first-letter` (CSS 2.1 s5.12.2). The member is a bit per
# PseudoElement saying whether any rule has ever named it, and the method is the question. It is
# here rather than in layout because it is a property of *the cascade* -- the same argument the
# invalidation index above is made from -- and it is what keeps the feature off the cost of every
# block box on every page whose sheets never mention it, which is nearly all of them.
budget: StyleResolver header_lines=200 public_methods=23 members=11
# The index itself. Two members and they are the two answers ADR 0016 §3
# separates: what any rule depends on, and what a rule that can move a box
# depends on. Collapsing them loses "a hover that changes only colour does not
# run layout", which is the second of the ADR's two properties.
budget: StyleInvalidation header_lines=45 public_methods=6 members=2
# One `@font-face`, as the sheet declares it (ADR 0024). A descriptor block rather
# than a rule: it matches nothing and styles nothing, it adds a face to the font
# database -- which is why it is a separate list on the sheet. Five members, and
# the fifth is a *bool* rather than the unicode-range text on purpose: the value
# arrives reconstructed from tokens and `U+0000-00FF` does not survive that, so
# keeping it would be keeping a lie.
budget: FontFace       header_lines=45 public_methods=3 members=6
budget: FontFaceSource header_lines=20 public_methods=2 members=2

# ADR 0014 §5's animation types. `AnimatableProperty` is the list of what this browser will interpolate
# and it is deliberately short -- a property on it that interpolated wrongly would be worse than one
# that is absent, so the list growing is a decision and the budget makes it visible.
# ADR 0014 §6's grid types. Grouped on ComputedStyle for the reason FlexStyle is: the container reads the
# tracks and the gaps, the item reads its placement, and loose they would say nothing about belonging to
# each other.
budget: GridStyle       header_lines=40 public_methods=2 members=12
budget: GridTrack       header_lines=30 public_methods=4 members=3
budget: GridPlacement   header_lines=25 public_methods=3 members=3
budget: AnimatableProperty header_lines=40 public_methods=0 members=0
budget: TimingFunction  header_lines=30 public_methods=0 members=7
budget: TransitionSpec  header_lines=25 public_methods=2 members=5
budget: AnimationSpec   header_lines=35 public_methods=2 members=8
budget: Keyframe        header_lines=15 public_methods=0 members=2
budget: KeyframesRule   header_lines=15 public_methods=0 members=2
budget: CssomRuleType   header_lines=20 public_methods=0 members=0
budget: CssomRule       header_lines=20 public_methods=0 members=6
# The seam the animation pass reaches the cascade through. One method, and it stays one: everything a
# frame needs to know is "here is a resolved style, adjust it".
budget: StyleAdjuster   header_lines=20 public_methods=3 members=0
